quiz:
  - q: 'A strategy has a small edge per trade. What happens to its total result as it makes more and more independent trades?'
    opts: ['The expected profit and the swings grow at the same rate, so the result never gets more certain', 'The expected profit grows in proportion to the number of trades but the swings only grow with its square root, so a profit becomes more and more certain', 'The edge wears off after a few thousand trades, so more trades make no difference']
    right: 1
    why: 'Expected profit is \(n \times\) edge, but the typical swing is about \(\sqrt{n}\) times the swing of one trade. Multiply the trades by \(100\) and the profit grows \(100\times\) while the swings grow only \(10\times\). That''s the law of large numbers at work, and it''s the reason quant firms trade so often.'
  - q: 'Which of these is algorithmic execution rather than a quant trading strategy?'
    opts: ['A rule that buys currencies whose prices have risen over the past year and sells those that have fallen', 'A computer that holds positions for a few seconds at a time to earn the bid-ask spread', 'A broker''s computer slicing a pension fund''s decided purchase of 2 million shares into small orders through the day']
    right: 2
    why: 'Execution algorithms decide how to carry out an order a person has already chosen. A quant strategy decides what to buy or sell in the first place. The second option is high-frequency market making, a kind of quant strategy.'
  - q: 'Why did the US switch to prices in cents in 2001 help quant trading grow?'
    opts: ['Smaller price steps squeezed spreads, so each trade earned less and the business rewarded trading a huge number of times very cheaply, which only machines can do', 'It made trading free for everyone', 'It banned human traders from the stock exchanges']
    right: 0
    why: 'The smallest price step fell from \(1/16\) of a dollar (\(6.25\) cents) to \(1\) cent. A spread worth a few cents a share can only pay for a business if you capture it millions of times, at low cost and with fast reactions: a job for software.'

